## 7. Assessment Ownership and Decision Roles

Stakeholder responsibilities are defined according to the role each group plays in current-state discovery, validation, and decision-readiness.

| Role / Stakeholder Group | Assessment Responsibility | Decision Role |
|---|---|---|
| Executive Leadership | Provide organizational direction and review consolidated findings | Decision authority for subsequent phases |
| Clinical Leadership | Validate clinical priorities, workflow considerations, criticality, and continuity concerns | Clinical leadership perspective on subsequent decisions |
| Clinical Users | Provide practitioner workflow knowledge and current-state observations | Workflow and usability validation |
| Clinical Engineering / Biomedical Engineering | Validate device information, lifecycle, maintenance, and support conditions | Technical device subject-matter expertise |
| IT Infrastructure / Networking | Validate network and infrastructure relationships and dependencies | Infrastructure subject-matter expertise |
| Integration / Interface Team | Validate interfaces, information flows, system relationships, and integration dependencies | Integration subject-matter expertise |
| Cybersecurity | Validate cybersecurity considerations, exposure, access, monitoring, and security dependencies | Cybersecurity subject-matter expertise |
| EHR / Clinical Systems Team | Validate EHR and clinical-system relationships and information exchange | Clinical-system subject-matter expertise |
| Vendor / Manufacturer Representatives | Provide vendor-specific product, lifecycle, support, and technical information | Vendor subject-matter information; not organizational decision authority |
| Technical Project Manager / Assessment Team | Coordinate assessment activities, evidence, stakeholders, risks, assumptions, dependencies, documentation, and decision-readiness | Assessment coordination; does not serve as technical, clinical, or cybersecurity authority |

Technical, clinical, operational, cybersecurity, and clinical-system conclusions should be validated with the appropriate subject-matter experts where required.

Modernization, procurement, remediation, implementation, and future-state architecture decisions are outside the scope of the current assessment.

